{
  "verdict": "REVISE",
  "summary": "Potential OOM on startup due to reading entire job log file into memory; recent() reads whole file regardless of requested tail size, and on_boot() calls recent(5000) which can be huge. No other critical bugs found.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "F1",
      "severity": "high",
      "path": "jobs.py",
      "issue": "Function recent() reads the entire LOG_FILE into memory before slicing the last N lines. on_boot() invokes recent(5000) on every startup, which can cause excessive memory usage if the log file has grown large before the trimming step.",
      "evidence": "def recent(n: int = 30):\n    try:\n        with open(LOG_FILE, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            lines = f.readlines()[-n:]\n    except Exception:\n        return []\n    ...\n\non_boot() calls rows = recent(5000) and later trims the file, but the initial read may already OOM.",
      "suggestion": "Implement a true tail read that only loads the last N lines (e.g., using a reverse file iterator or reading in chunks). Also consider limiting the size of recent() calls or using a rotating log to keep the file bounded."
    }]}

